Fixing use of std::min/max, make sure first arg can never be NaN/-NaN

Summary:
This diff addresses the failure in the PyTorch build mentioned here: D19151354

We make sure the first arg to max/min is not NAN/-NAN, This is because when it comes to NANs, min/max is not commutative
Eg. max(-NAN, d=4.0f) != max(d=4.0f, -NAN) fixing the issue illustrated by the undefined behavior here:
https://godbolt.org/z/dEuTgj

Input:
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;
int main()
{
    float a = -NAN;
    float b = std::numeric_limits<float>::min();
    float c = 5.0f;
    float d = 4.0f;
    std::cout << std::max(b, a) << std::endl;
    std::cout << std::max(a, b) << std::endl;
    std::cout << std::min(std::max(a, b),c) << std::endl;
    std::cout << std::min(c, std::max(a, b)) << std::endl;
    return 1;
}

Output:
Program stdout
1.17549e-38
-nan
-nan
5

